    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d913">

                <pb facs="0987" n="701" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg701"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111So4/71</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The French Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Jusserand</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-12-30">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 30, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received January 3,
                        1916</hi>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: The information mentioned
                    in your letter of the 28th concerning Mr. Thaw, Mr.
                        Cowdin and Mr. Prince was known to
                    me, but, as you surmised, only from the papers. I cabled at once to my
                    Government making them aware of your request.</p>
                <p>From what I hear, and this I have from quite trustworthy authority, the motive of
                    their coming, for the briefest stay in the United States, was a desire to see
                    their relatives in this season, and they had no other.</p>
                <p>Believe me [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jusserand</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
